## ABSTRACT
We report the use of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) for the selective separation of nucleic acids (DNA and RNA) with different secondary structure through size, shape, length and capability of conformational transition. Three MOFs with precisely controlled pore environment, Co-IRMOF-74-II, -III, and -IV, composed of $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ and organic linkers (II, III and IV), respectively, were used for inclusion of nucleic acid into their pores from the solution. This was proven to be a spontaneous process from disordered free state to restricted ordered state via circular dichroism (CD) spectroscopy. Three critical factors were identified for their inclusion: 1) size selection induced by steric hindrance, 2) conformation transition energy selection induced by stability, and 3) molecular weight selection. These selection rules were used to extract nucleic acids with flexible and unstable secondary structure from complex mixtures of multiple nucleic acids, leaving those with rigid and stable secondary structure in the mother liquor. This provides the possibility to separate and enrich nucleic acids in bulk through their different structure feature, which is highly desirable in genome-wide structural measurement of nucleic acids. Unlike methods rely on specific binding antibodies or ligand, this MOF method is capable of selecting all kinds of nucleic acids with similar secondary structure feature, therefore it is suitable for handling of a large variety and quantity of nucleic acids at the same time. This method also has the potential to gather information about the folding stability of biomolecules with secondary structure.
## INTRODUCTION
Majority of the nucleic acids, deoxynucleic acid (DNA) and ribonucleic acid (RNA), are flexible in single strand form in solution, but some of them can form various rigid secondary structures, such as double helix, G-quadruplex (G4)1-4, hairpin, triple helix, and i-motif structure. The secondary structure of RNA molecules is essential for their biological functions and cellular regulations5,6. Conformation dynamic changes in RNA in response to change in intracellular conditions are the basis of RNA regulatory mechanism and the origin of their complex functionality7. RNA molecules are capable of transformation between different secondary structures8,9, making it difficult to extract such structural information, especially when a large amount and variety of RNA molecules coexist. Structure-specific antibodies10,11 and structure-binding ligands12 have been used to target a specific structure feature of RNA molecules to achieve isolation, however they are only applicable to a limited RNA type at one time, while it is difficult to obtain the overall information of the secondary structures. Electrophoretic mobility shift assay (EMSA)13,14, DMS footprinting15 and the DNA polymerase stop assay16 are successfully applied to recognize a broad range of RNA molecules. As the demand increasing on genome-wide structural measurement of nucleic acids in both research and industry, it still remains challenging to meet the requirement of handling samples with complex structure variety and large sample amount. This is mainly due to the lack of efficient means to separate samples based on their secondary structure, resulting in high background signal in the RNA sequencing process.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), a class of porous crystalline materials with molecularly defined pore environment, are ideally suited for recognition and separation of molecules in large quantity. MOFs have been applied in the separation of small molecules 17,18, storage of gases 19-21, catalysis22, imaging23,24, and drug delivery25-27, however, the study on their interaction with large biological molecules is just emerging. Our previous study has shown that mesoporous MOFs are capable of introducing single strand DNA without secondary structure inside their pores rather than on the surface of
MOF28, while the study on DNA and RNA with specific secondary structures in MOFs remained unknown. Unveiling interaction between DNA and RNA with specific secondary structures and MOFs is likely to provide information on the secondary structures in these nucleic acids, and potentially leads to a new method for their efficient separation.
In this study, the function of mesoporous MOFs to nucleic acids with different secondary structures was investigated, revealing three critical factors for their separation: 1) size matching selection, 2) molecular weight selection and 3) stability selection during the adsorption process (Figure 1). Compared with nucleic acids with small cross-section and flexible single-stranded, these nucleic acid molecules with rigid secondary structures exhibit larger cross-section, thus enduring higher space resistance when entering narrow MOF pores (Figure 1A). Furthermore, nucleic acids of similar structure but with smaller molecular weight can facilely enter the pores of MOFs due to their higher diffusion rate at the interface (Figure 1B). In addition, nucleic acids with secondary structure of the size bigger than pore aperture of MOFs can still enter their pores through change in molecular conformations (Figure 1C). DNA and RNA with stable secondary structure is harder to unfold into single strand, thus they are more difficult to enter the pores of MOFs (Figure 1C). Based on these selection rules, nucleic acids with single strand or unstable secondary structure are preferentially included by MOF materials, leaving the ones with stable secondary structure in the supernatant. This method is suitable to differentiate a large variety of nucleic acids based on their secondary structures, standing as a new way for the separation and enrichment of nucleic acids with secondary structures. It also exhibits great potential in genome-wide structural measurement of nucleic acids due to its capability to handle complex structure variety and large sample amount.
## RESULT
## Synthesis, characterization and stability study of MOF materials
Based on the crystal structure of MOF-74, a robust framework with hexagonal topology (etb net) and one dimensional (1D) pores, the gradual pore size extension was implemented by inserting multiple phenylene units into the original 2,5- dioxidoterephthalate to prepare organic linkers with different length (linker-II, -III and -IV). These linkers were coordinated with a divalent metal $(\mathrm{Co}^{2+})$ through multiple oxygen atoms to build a series of three isoreticular MOFs, which are termed as CoIRMOF-74-II, -III, and -IV, respectively (Figure 2E). The high crystallinity of CoIRMOF-74-II, -III, and -IV was evidenced by their sharp peaks in the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns (Figure S1-3) that match well with the simulated patterns based on the parent MOF and Ni analogs as reported5.
The permanent porosity of the Co-IRMOF-74 series was further confirmed by $\Nu_{2}$ gas adsorption at 77 K (Figure S4-6). Co-IRMOF-74-II exhibits a Type I isotherm due to smaller pore size, while Co-IRMOF-74-III and -IV exhibit a Type IV isotherm, typical for mesoporous materials. Through quenched solid density function theory (QSDFT), the calculated pore sizes of these Co-IRMOFs were found to be around 1.8 nm, $2.4~\mathrm{nm}$ , and $3.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. These values are compatible with the gradual increase of the diagonal diameters in their crystal structures and matched well with the pore size distribution of reported Ni-IRMOF-745. These MOFs inherit the excellent chemical stability of Ni-IRMOF-74 series, which is demonstrated by the sharp peaks after immersion in various buffer solution ( $100\mathrm{mM}$ KCl buffered with $20~\mathrm{mM}$ KAc at $\mathrm{pH}6.8$ and 50 mM KCl buffered with 20 mM KAc at $\mathrm{pH}5.4$ and 6.8) (Figure S7-9).
Inclusion of DNA molecules with different structural features into MOF with progressively tuned pore size
In this study, the quantification of ssDNA inclusion into the pores of the MOF samples was achieved by labeling the $5^{\circ}$ end of each DNA with 6-carboxyfluorescein (FAM), giving green fluorescence at a wavelength of $520\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The decay of the fluorescence in the solution indicate the amount of the DNA uptake into the pore of MOF materials. After different types of DNA were annealed in solution in advance to form their structure, the equivalent amount of DNA reacted with MOF material respectively, and the fluorescence intensity in the supernatant was tested.
G-quadruplex adsorption. G-quadruplex is classified into three different types based on glycosidic bond angles (GBA, including anti and syn type) adopted by guanosines of the G-quadruplex stem as previous reported29. It has been proved that DNA can form G4 structure not only in vitro but also in vivo, and the existence of G4 structure has biological significance11, 30,31.
Linear DNA (M-1XAV, M-2GKU, M-2FK8) are mutant of G4 sequence, these are a loose and flexible single stand DNA (Figure S46-48), and the structure of linear sequence and G-quadruplex sequence was evidenced by characteristic absorption peak in circular dichroism (CD) spectra and melting curve (Figure S22-S27). MOF materials (Co-IRMOF-74-II, III, and IV) can include more than $90\%$ of this type of linear DNA, while the adsorption efficiency of three MOF for three different types of G4 structures is significantly less than that of linear DNA (Figure 2F), suggesting that the formation of G4 structure prevents DNA from entering MOF pores. However, the adsorption efficiency of MOF materials to distinct structural DNA is different, and directly related to their stability. Group I-1XAV has the highest melting temperature (Tm) value among the six selected G4 sequences, that is to say, the highest stability (Figure S27). The adsorption efficiency of three materials, Co-IRMOF-74-II, III and IV, is $20\%$ , $40\%$ and $0\%$ , respectively. The second highest stability is II-11a, the adsorption efficiency of the above three materials is $40\%$ , $80\%$ and $40\%$ , respectively.
Double strand DNA adsorption. Adsorption efficiencies of three kinds of MOF materials for double strand DNA (dsDNA) were also evaluated, result showing that adsorption efficiency of Co-IRMOF-74-II, III and IV to dsDNA is $20\%$ , $95\%$ and $80\%$ , respectively (Figure 2F). Co-IRMOF-74-II has the worst ability to include doublestranded DNA, indicating that double-stranded DNA cannot enter the aperture of CoIRMOF-74-II, but it can be included by Co-IRMOF-74-III and IV, the reasons for this difference in adsorption capacity is the difference steric hindrance entering into the three kinds of MOF materials.
## Inclusion of RNA with different structural features
Although chemically distinct with DNA, RNA can also fold into G-quadruplex structures in vitro, and that are highly stable because of the $2^{\circ}$ -hydroxyl group. Biophysical studies have proved that RNA G-quadruplexes formed in vitro are thermodynamically more stable than their DNA counterparts under near-physiological conditions $^{32,33}$ . Furthermore, DNA G-quadruplex forms these three types of G4 structures，but RNA G-quadruplexes sequence predominately form a parallel topology upon addition of $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions because of the $2^{\circ}$ -hydroxyl group34-36.
To identify the selective inclusion for different structural RNA, the uptake efficiency of the MOF materials to four distinct structure RNA sequence was tested. The telomeric repeat containing RNA G-quadruplex (TERRA), a G-quadruplex in the $5^{\circ}$ -UTR (NRAS), single strand RNA(G4 mutant sequence, MUT-TERRA), hairpin RNA ( stem: 7 paired bases) were used in this study，two of the G4 has previously been shown to fold into an intramolecular G-quadruplex37. CD spectra of the TERRA and NARS give a characteristic positive peak near $265~\mathrm{nm}$ and a negative peak near $240~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S29), indicated that the polarity of all the strands is oriented in one direction, that means forming parallel G4. The adsorption efficiency of three MOF materials for MUT-TERRA, hairpin RNA, TERRA and NARS were more than $70\%$ , $50\%-60\%$ , $40\%-0$ and $30\%-0$ , respectively. Among them, the adsorption efficiency of $\mathrm{Co}$ -IRMOF-74-IV for MUT-TERRA is as high as $80\%$ , for hairpin RNA is $60\%$ , and for G4 sequence TERRA and NARS is basically negligible (Figure 2F). Thermodynamic research was carried out to investigate the stability of the four sequences, as showed in Figure S30, G4 structure (TERRA and NARS) process a higher melting temperature ( $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $95^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) than hairpin structure (about $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and single strand RNA. Compared with DNA hairpin structure (adsorption efficiency: about $90\%$ ) with seven paired bases, the adsorption efficiency of MOF to RNA hairpin structure with the same number of paired bases is lower $(50-60\%$ than that of DNA, because the hairpin structure of RNA is more stable than that of DNA under the same number of paired bases. In conclusion, the structure of stability and adsorption efficiency test shows that it is more difficult for RNA with secondary structure to enter the pores of MOF, which is consistent with the results of DNA and MOF adsorption test.
## Inclusion of single strand DNA
We compared the adsorption efficiency of Co-IRMOF-74-I, -II, -III and -IV on ssDNA (M-143D, 22 nucleotides), founding that the adsorption efficiency of CoIRMOF-74-II, -III and -IV on ssDNA was over $95\%$ , however, the adsorption efficiency of Co-IRMOF-74-I on ssDNA was only $20\%$ (Figure 2G). The Co-IRMOF74-I, -II, -III and -IV materials have the same topological structure, but their pore size increases in turn (Table 1). If only surface adsorption, the adsorption efficiency of these materials to ssDNA should be the same; however, in fact, the Co-IRMOF-74-I material with the smallest pore size has the worst adsorption efficiency to ssDNA $(20\%)$ . The results show that ssDNA bears great space resistance when entering into Co-IRMOF74-I channel, only a small amount of surface adsorption.
## Circular dichroism study of ssDNA and MOF complex
CD may be regarded as one of the most powerful techniques for stereochemical analysis, which provides information on the overall molecular stereochemistry 38. However, the theoretical description of CD spectra of DNA is very complicated, CD spectroscopy is only primarily used empirically in studies of DNA structure39. Previous study reported that CD spectrum of DNA driven into a compact state undergoing a spontaneous rearrangement to an ordered tertiary structure is greatly different from that of DNA in solution40. In this study, the CD spectra of pure MOF, linear DNA (Mut143D) and complex formed by Mut-143D and CO-IRMOF-74-II were tested, the results showing that CD adsorption peaks did not observed in CO-IRMOF-74-II, and the DNA-MOF complex produced obvious new peaks (negative peaks at $375~\mathrm{nm}$ and positive peaks at $335~\mathrm{nm}$ ) that are different from pure linear DNA (M-143D) (Figure S28). This indicates that MOF materials have interacted with DNA, and the conformation of DNA existing in MOF pores has changed. The process of DNA entering MOF narrow pores from solution is a spontaneous process from disordered free state to restricted ordered state.
## Inclusion of dsDNA/ssDNA with different lengths
In order to test the influence of length or molecular weight on the adsorption process, we chose Co-IRMOF-74-IV as the research object, designed a series of dsDNA (from 10 bp-46 bp, bp means base pared) and ssDNA (from 10 nt – 46 nt, nt means nucleotides) with different length, and then tested the adsorption efficiency of Co-IRMOF-74-IV on these sequences. As shown in Figure 2H, the adsorption efficiency of both ssDNA and dsDNA decreases with the increase of their chain length (molecular weight); for the same length of ssDNA and dsDNA, the adsorption efficiency of Co-IRMOF-74-IV for ssDNA is higher than that of dsDNA. The results of electrophoresis (Figure S42) showed that two of strand of the dsDNA completely complemented each other to form a double stranded structure. This result also proves that the molecular weight has an important influence on the adsorption process.
## Inclusion of mismatched dsDNA with different Tm
In order to confirm the correlation between structure stability and sequestration into MOF materials, we designed a series of double stranded DNA sequences (22nt) with different number of mismatched bases, and then evaluated the adsorption efficiency of Co-IRMOF-74-II on this series of double stranded DNA with different stability. The number of mismatched bases ranges from 0 to 5, and the sequence of dsDNA is shown in the Table S4. Through the test of circular dichroic spectrum and melting curve, Tm value is obtained by simulating and calculating the melting curve, as shown in Figure 3B. The Tm value of dsDNA with complete complementary pairing was the highest, reaching $53^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . As the number of mismatched bases increases, the Tm value decreases gradually. When the number of mismatched bases reaches 5, the Tm is only $19\ {^\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , indicating that its double chain structure is very unstable at room temperature.
The adsorption efficiency of MOF on dsDNA was tested by fluorescence experiment, as shown in Figure 3A, the adsorption efficiency of dsDNA with complete complementary pairing is only $25\%$ . The adsorption efficiency of dsDNA with 1 to 5 mismatched bases increases in turn, from about $60\%$ to $80\%$ . However, the adsorption efficiency of ssDNA with the same sequence is more than $95\%$ . In addition, we detected the existence state of DNA in the supernatant after adsorption by electrophoresis. As shown in Figure S41, when the mismatch base was in the range of 0 to 3, most of the DNA was double strand state at room temperature, while the number of mismatched bases increased to 5, there was a small number of ssDNA forms, which was exactly the same as we expected.
The diameter of cross-sectional area of dsDNA is larger than the pores size of CoIRMOF-74-II, so it is very difficult to enter into the inner channel without the configuration transformation of dsDNA. Because of the exist of mismatch in the dsDNA, their stability decreased with the increasing number of mismatches. Therefore, dsDNA with more mismatch numbers is easier to change its configuration to enter the channel of IRMOF-74-II, resulting that the uptake efficiency of IRMOF-74-II for unstable dsDNA is higher than stable dsDNA.
## Inclusion of hairpin DNA with different structural stability
In order to further confirm that the adsorption efficiency of MOF materials to structural DNA is directly related to their stability, the adsorption efficiencies of MOF materials to three different hairpin structure DNA were tested. The loop sequence of the three hairpin DNA is same, while the only difference is that the number of paired bases in stem region increases gradually, the number of paired bases is 5, 7, 9, named S5, S7 and S9, respectively. Theoretically, it is commonly known that the greater number of paired bases in stem, the more stable it is (stability ${\bf S}9>{\bf S}7>{\bf S}5$ ). It was also confirmed by the tests of circular dichroism and melting curve, Tm of S5 and S7 is about $45^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , Tm of S9 is $65^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 3D, S31 and S32). The adsorption efficiency of Co-IRMOF-74-II for S9, S7 and S5 is $20\%$ , $90\%$ , $90\%$ , and that of Co-IRMOF-74- IV is $40\%$ , $95\%$ and $98\%$ (Figure 3C.). The adsorption efficiency for S9 is lower than S5 and S7, because the stability of S9 is higher than S7 and S5.
## Inclusion of i-motif DNA at different pH condition
I-motif is a sensitive pH-triggered self-assembly secondary structure of DNA41. The C-rich telomeric strand of vertebrates, $\mathrm{{d}[\mathrm{{CCCTAA}]_{3}C C C}}$ , denoted Oligo-i-motif was used in this study, which is confirmed to fold into an intramolecular tetraplex structure with intercalated Cytidine– $\mathrm{\cdotH^{+}}.$ –Cytidine base pairs at slightly acid or neutral pH and the stability of the i-motif is pH-dependent42. In general, at $\mathsf{p H}$ above 4.5, the stability of the i-motifs decreases with increasing $\mathrm{pH}^{43,44}$ .
To verify the correlation between the i-motif structure and changes in pH, circular dichroism spectroscopy and thermodynamic research were used to test the conformation and stability of i-motif structure. CD spectra give a strong positive band near $290\mathrm{nm}$ and a negative band at approximately 260 nm in pH 5.2 - 6.1(Figure S33), providing direct evidence of the existence of the DNA i-motif in these pH conditions. Besides, the melting temperature of the i-motif increased with the decreasing pH (from 6.0 to 5.2), as showed in Figure 3F and S34, and melting temperature of i-motif increased with the decrease of $\mathrm{pH}$ value from 6.0 to 5.2, indicating that the i-motif structure is more stable under the acid condition. The adsorption efficiency of CoIRMOF-IV on i-motif increases with the increase of $\mathfrak{p H}$ , but the adsorption efficiency of mutant i-motif sequence is basically unchanged under different pH conditions (the adsorption efficiency is above $80\%$ ). This indicates that the adsorption of i-motif by MOF is directly related to its stability.
In this experiment, the same sequence was tested under different pH conditions, excluding the influence of different sequence and length on the adsorption efficiency, and the adsorption efficiency of non-i-motif sequence at different pH was also tested, showing that the different pH did not affect the adsorption capacity of MOF materials.
So, in this experiment, only the stability of i-motif structure is the factor that affects the adsorption efficiency. The results show that with the stability of i-motif structure getting worse, the adsorption efficiency of MOF gradually increases, which proves the negative influence of DNA stability on the adsorption process. What's more, when the difference of Tm value is very small and only a few degrees, the adsorption efficiency is obviously different. It shows that MOF has the ability to reflect the structural stability of nucleic acids, and it can reflect small differences.
Spatial size study of G4 structure, dsDNA, i-motif and MOFs based on their crystal structures
In order to explain why MOF has different adsorptive capacity for DNA/RNA with different structures, we studied the crystal structures of MOF and secondary structural nucleic acids, and tried to explore spatial size how to affect the interaction mode between nucleic acids and MOF materials. The secondary structures of nucleic acids are based on the crystal structures of dsDNA (PDB entry 6GPI)45, parallel DNA Gquadruplex (PDB entry 2M27)46, parallel RNAG-quadruplex (PDB entry 2AWE)47, and i-motif structure (PDB entry 5OGA)48. The width of double-stranded DNA is about 2 nm (Figure 2A), the pore sizes of these three materials are $2.0{\times}2.2$ , $2.3{\times}2.9$ , $2.7{\times}3.5$ nm (Figure 2E). The diameter of dsDNA is close to the pore size of Co-IRMOF-74-II, smaller than that of Co-IRMOF-74-III. Therefore, it is difficult for dsDNA to enter CoIRMOF-74-II. It is relatively easier for dsDNA to enter Co-IRMOF-74-III and IV. By analyzing the crystal structure documents of G4, we find that the diameter of the rigid stem plane formed by parallel DNA/RNA G-quadruplex is about $3.4\AA-3.6~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 2B and 2C), diameter of i-motif is $3.2\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 2D), these all larger than aperture of CO-IRMOF-74-II, III and IV.
Therefore, it is difficult for DNA/RNA G4, i-motif structure to enter the aperture of these three materials from the point of view of steric hindrance, showing that MOF has size matching effect on structural nucleic acids adsorption, and MOF has no capacity to include nucleic acids larger than the pore size of MOF. This is supported by evidence as followed: a) the uptake efficiency of dsDNA by Co-IRMOF-74-II, III and IV is $20\%$ , $95\%$ and $80\%$ , respectively (Figure 2F). The cross-sectional size of doublestranded DNA is similar to that of Co-IRMOF-74-II, smaller than that of Co-IRMOF74-III and IV. Therefore, it is very difficult for double-stranded DNA to enter into the pores of Co-IRMOF-74-II, and it is easier for double-stranded DNA to enter into the pores of Co-IRMOF-74-III and IV. b) The adsorption efficiencies for larger crosssectional structural nucleic acid (DNA G4, RAN G4, i-motif) were lower than singlestranded DNA/RNA by MOF materials, as showed in Figure 2F and 3E, respectively. c) Adsorption efficiency of Co-IRMOF-74-II, -III and -IV for ssDNA was over $95\%$ , however, the adsorption efficiency of Co-IRMOF-74-I for ssDNA was only $20\%$ (Figure 2G). The pore size of Co-IRMOF-74-I is the smallest among these Co-IRMOF74 series (Table 1), leading to ssDNA bearing huge steric hindrance in the process of entering into their pores.
## Selective inclusion of mixed nucleic acids with different structures
In order to evaluate the ability of MOF materials to selectively include different structural DNA under complicated mixture condition, we prepared two mixed DNA samples, one is mixed with G4 (I-1XAV), linear DNA (MUT-I-1XAV), hairpin DNA (S7), dsDNA (ds-MUT-143D), and the other is linear DNA (DZ) instead of linear DNA (MUT-I-1XAV). MUT-I-1XAV and DZ are loose single-chain states, the chain length was 22 nucleotides (nt) and 33nt, respectively. For mixed sample 1, Co-IRMOF-74-III, IV can basically include linear DNA (MUT-I-1XAV), hairpin DNA (S7), dsDNA (MUT-143D), leaving only G4 (I-1XAV) in solution. Co-IRMOF-74-II can include linear DNA (MUT-I-1XAV), hairpin DNA (S7) completely and leave dsDNA (MUT143D) and G4 (I-1XAV) in solution (Figure 4A). The results are in good agreement with the adsorption efficiency of MOF materials for single species of DNA with different structures. Selective adsorption of mixed sample 2 are similar to those of mixed sample 1, which includes almost all the DNA with free loose state (linear DNA) and unstable structure (hairpin DNA (S7)). However, compared with mixed sample 1, in the test of sample 2, Co-IRMOF-74-II has a weaker ability to include linear DNA (DZ) than linear DNA (MUT-I-1XAV) with a shorter chain length, indicating that increasing the length of DNA strands will prevent DNA from entering the pores of MOF materials.
In order to further illustrate the selective adsorption ability of MOF to different RNA structures under complicated condition, mixed samples of MUT-TERRA, NARS and hairpin was prepared. Three MOF materials can include single strand RNA from solution into pores, and leave RNA G4 and hairpin RNA in solution (Figure 4B).
## DISCUSSION
In this study, we have proved that the adsorption process is physical adsorption through weak interaction, excluding electrostatic and coordination interaction through testing Zeta potential and XPS spectra (Figure S10-S18). The driving force of this adsorption process is likely to originate from the surface tension and excess surface Gibbs free energy at the molecular interface of MOFs49. Owing to the high specific surface area of MOF materials, it favors the capture of the included gas molecules and liquid molecules, which is the feature of porous materials with high adsorption capacity50-52. Importantly, the factors that affect the adsorption process of mesoporous crystal MOF materials and nucleic acids with different secondary structures was discussed in terms of steric hindrance, entropy and energy (Figure 1), indicating that MOFs selectively included nucleic acids depend on their different size/ shape, length/molecular weight, and structural stability.
Firstly, size matching between the pores of MOF materials and structural nucleic acids is the prerequisite for whether MOF can include nucleic acids. Ordered mesoporous crystals Co-IRMOF-74-II, -III, and -IV materials have adjustable pores size $(2.0{\times}2.2,2.3{\times}2.9,2.7{\times}3.5\ \mathrm{nm}_{\cdot}$ , Figure 2E), the diameter of cross-sectional area of double-stranded DNA is about $2\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 2A), dsDNA has a rigid structure, while ssDNA exists in an irregular and flexible state in solution, the diameter of crosssectional area of other structural DNA/RNA was summarized in the Table 1. Compared the size of MOF and nucleic acids, it could be found that the nucleic acids with smaller cross-section enter into the pores of MOF unimpededly, while rigid nucleic acids with larger cross-section enter into the pores of MOF materials bearing stronger spatial resistance. For example, Co-IRMOF-74-I has a low adsorption capacity for ssDNA due to its small pore size. Co-IRMOF-74-II can well include ssDNA, but its adsorption capacity for dsDNA is very weak (Figure 2F and 3G).
Secondly, after the size matching is satisfied, the molecular weight of DNA affects the adsorption efficiency. As shown in Figure 2H. the adsorption efficiency of dsDNA decreases with the increase of their chain length (from 10 bp to $46~\mathrm{bp}$ ); for the same length of ssDNA and dsDNA, the adsorption efficiency of Co-IRMOF-74-IV for ssDNA is higher than that of dsDNA. Previous studies showed that passage of polymers through narrow pores is impeded by an entropic penalty for their confinement 53-56. This might be balanced by surface energy from the pore walls57. In this entropy reduction process, smaller solute molecules, with the other properties identical, are more likely to achieve confinement in MOF pores due to the higher diffuse rate, as the reduction in molecular weight favors the adsorption of smaller polymer on surface58. Similar trend is observed here, when biopolymeric molecules, ssDNA and dsDNA, were included into the pores of MOF materials. DNA molecules with small molecular weight exhibit higher uptake efficiency in MOFs, likely due to higher diffusion rate at the pore interface.
Thirdly, MOF materials have higher adsorption efficiency for unstable DNA /RNA molecules under the condition of size mismatch and the same molecular weight, suggesting that unstable rigid structural nucleic acids are able to change their conformations to enter into the pore of MOF materials under strong spatial resistance. The force of stabilizing the secondary structure of nucleic acid is weak interaction force, such as H-bond, $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking, hydrophobic interaction, resulting that the secondary structure of nucleic acids can be destroyed in the presence of strong interaction force, for example, under the condition of heating, under the condition of changing pH or changing ionic strength. The excess Gibbs free energy on the surface of MOF materials provides energy, which enables DNA/RNA molecules with secondary structure to change their configuration to overcome the space resistance bearing in the process of entering the pore. The energy required for the configuration transformation of DNA/RNA molecular with poor stability is low, so more unstable DNA molecules undergo configuration transformation to enter the MOF pore, Macroscopically, MOF has a higher adsorption efficiency for molecules with poor stability. These three factors together affect the adsorption process of DNA/RNA by MOF, and finally reach the adsorption equilibrium state.
Last but not least, under complicated condition mixed various structural nucleic acids, three MOF materials show excellent selective adsorption capacity. Three MOF materials includes almost all the linear DNA and unstable structure (hairpin DNA: S7), leaving DNA G4 in the solution (Figure 4A); Besides, three MOF materials can include single strand RNA from solution into the pores, leaving RNA G4 and hairpin RNA in solution coexisting none rigid structure and rigid structural RNA (Figure 4B). It suggests that MOF materials are capable to selectively include nucleic acids with nonerigid structure or unstable rigid structure, leaving stable secondary structural nucleic acids in the solution. The enriched stable structural nucleic acid can be collected to characterize the structure by sequencing analysis, indicating that MOF materials has a great potential for application in the field of enrichment and monitoring all type of structural nucleic acids.
## CONCLUSION
In summary, the main issue addressed in the present research is that we confirmed the factors influencing the absorption process of mesoporous crystal MOF materials and nucleic acids with different secondary structures. MOFs selectively included nucleic acids through their different size/ shape, length/molecular weight, and structural stability. Generally, due to the large specific surface area of MOF, the current researches focus on the storage and separation of gas, organic small molecules. However, MOF materials are rarely used to include and separate biomacromolecules, because the adsorption mode of MOF to biomacromolecules is poorly understood. This work provides a good beginning, leading to the adsorption and separation ability of MOF materials can be extended to the field of biomacromolecules. On the other hand, MOF has a good adsorption efficiency for nucleic acids, especially the ability to selectively include nucleic acids with different structures. This is a great breakthrough in the field of nucleic acid separation and enrichment with different structures, owing to the main method now depends on the separation of specific binding antibodies, especially in the field of monitoring all structural RNA due to the dynamic change of
RNA conformations. In addition, MOF is very sensitive to nucleic acid molecules with different stability, fine stability differences can be reflected by the adsorption efficiency, indicating that MOF has the potential to act as a method to indirectly gather information about biomolecular structural stability.
